<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The RSVPâ€“CPG framework extends this embodied lineage into a neurocomputational theory of consciousness. Building on recent evidence that cortical networks activate in robust cyclical sequences (Van Es et al., 2025), we propose that consciousness itself is a gait. Cortical cycles function as chained central pattern generators, carrying perception, memory, and action forward in rhythmic order. Memory persists through proxy loops entrained to this gait, while paradoxical sleep arises when the chains desynchronize. By situating RSVPâ€“CPG in the tradition of peripatetic philosophy and in dialogue with contemporary accounts of embodied cognition, we suggest that the oscillatory skeleton uncovered in neuroimaging is not an incidental correlate but the fundamental form of thought.</p>
