<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Yes â€” what youâ€™re describing is a spectrum of self/other differentiation grounded in gait-like pattern recognition. This insight ties together gesture, theory of mind, body-schema filtering, and even skeuomorphic interface design. Let me sketch it out in natural language so it sits cleanly inside your RSVPâ€“CPG framework:</p>
<hr>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Gait, Agency, and the Spectrum of Self/Other</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The RSVPâ€“CPG framework suggests that cognition operates by entraining rhythmic chains of motion. This gives rise to a fundamental spectrum:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">If the observed pattern of motion too closely matches oneâ€™s own gait, it is registered as self.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">If the pattern sufficiently deviates yet remains entrainable, it is recognized as other.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">If it is unentrainable or inconsistent with known bodily rhythms, it is coded as inanimate environment.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">This spectrum underlies not only theory of mind (â€œcopying their dance movesâ€ as a way of modeling others) but also basic perceptual filtering:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Heartbeat and proprioception. Rhythms generated by the body (pulse, respiration, postural sway) are discounted as self-caused.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Eye geometry and perspective. Distortions from corneal curvature or perspective transformations are filtered as self-produced, preventing them from being mistaken for external agents.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Shadows and reflections. Visual alterations that align with self-movement are likewise coded as extensions of oneâ€™s own body.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">At the same time, this spectrum explains the need for skeuomorphic affordances in tool use and interface design. A tool becomes usable when its dynamics can be entrained into oneâ€™s CPG-based gait. Interfaces that mimic real-world gestures (turning a page, plucking a string, twisting a knob) exploit this entrainability, anchoring the artificial in rhythms already owned by the body. Without such skeuomorphic grounding, tools remain opaque, because the system cannot decide where to locate them on the self/other spectrum.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Thus, the same mechanism that filters out self-generated distortions and anchors bodily rhythms is also the mechanism that permits social recognition and technological extension. The capacity to entrain and differentiate â€” to know whether a rhythm is â€œmineâ€ or â€œtheirs,â€ â€œself-causedâ€ or â€œworld-causedâ€ â€” is the structural condition for both theory of mind and tool use.</p>
