<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Would you like me to make this into a formal subsection in your manuscript â€” something like â€œThe Self/Other Spectrum: From Perceptual Filtering to Tool Affordancesâ€ â€” and embed references to body-schema neuroscience (Gallagher, Blanke, Rizzolatti) and HCI skeuomorphism (Norman, Gibson)?</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Below is a compact synthesis that recasts your self/other spectrum, gait entrainment, and skeuomorphic affordances in the language of Markov blankets and Active Inference (AIF)â€”and shows why physics modeling is, in form, theory-of-mind (ToM) inference.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Markov blankets, gait, and the self/other spectrum</p>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="break-words whitespace-pre-wrap [&amp;&gt;ul]:whitespace-normal [&amp;&gt;ol]:whitespace-normal">The blanket partition</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">In AIF, a system is factorized by a Markov blanket into:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Internal states  (beliefs over hidden causes),</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Active states  (actions),</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Sensory states  (observations),</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">External states  (world causes).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Conditional independencies: . Inference updates  to minimize variational free energy , while actions  change  to fulfill predictions (policies).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">RSVPâ€“CPG view: your bodyâ€™s gait generators (CPGs) supply strong priors over lawful sensorimotor rhythms (heartbeat, respiration, postural sway, ocular micro-motion). These priors define a tight sensorimotor loopâ€”i.e., a self-blanket with high predictability and short latency between .</p>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="2">
<li class="break-words whitespace-pre-wrap [&amp;&gt;ul]:whitespace-normal [&amp;&gt;ol]:whitespace-normal">Self/other discrimination as synchrony-based blanket assignment</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Self: If an observed pattern is phase-locked to predicted consequences of your actions (or canonical interoceptive rhythms), its prediction error is low under the self model . It is thus absorbed inside the self-blanket.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Other/World: If a pattern is entrainable but not locked (phase-lead/lag outside self priors), it better fits an external generative model . It remains beyond the blanket but is modeled as a structured cause (agent or object).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Inanimate: If a pattern is unentrainable by any plausible policy, it is explained as passive dynamics (e.g., inertial, elastic, optical laws).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Formally, the system compares model evidences (negative free energies) for competing blankets:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\Delta\mathcal{F} \approx \mathcal{F}<span>*{\text{self}} - \mathcal{F}*</span>{\text{other/object}},</p>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="3">
<li class="break-words whitespace-pre-wrap [&amp;&gt;ul]:whitespace-normal [&amp;&gt;ol]:whitespace-normal">Why physics modeling is ToM in disguise</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Both ToM and intuitive physics are inference over external generative processes behind . The difference is parametric:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">ToM model: external states include latent goals, beliefs, policies . Likelihoods encode goal-consistent trajectories and policy-induced kinematics.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Physics model: external states include latent forces, constraints, media . Likelihoods encode law-consistent trajectories (Hamiltonian/Lagrangian constraints).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Both are structured priors over motion; both predict future phase evolution of observable states. Hence, ToM and physics are the same inference problem over different prior families. In practice, humans often adopt an intentional stance for complex dynamics because agentive priors can be lower-complexity descriptions for certain patterns (and vice versa for simple mechanics).</p>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="4">
<li class="break-words whitespace-pre-wrap [&amp;&gt;ul]:whitespace-normal [&amp;&gt;ol]:whitespace-normal">Filtering self-caused distortions via the self model</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Cardiac/respiratory pulses, ocular distortion, perspective warps, self-shadow/reflection: these are predicted under the self generative model  and thus explained away (low precision assigned to their prediction errors).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Failure modes (e.g., rubber-hand illusion, vertigo, alien-hand phenomena) occur when precision weighting misassigns signals to the wrong blanket, shifting boundaries between self and world.</p>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="5">
<li class="break-words whitespace-pre-wrap [&amp;&gt;ul]:whitespace-normal [&amp;&gt;ol]:whitespace-normal">Skeuomorphic affordances as blanket extensions</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Tools and interfaces become intuitive when their sensorimotor contingencies are homeomorphic to existing self policies: twisting a virtual knob, dragging a slider, turning a page. Skeuomorphism lowers  by:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Preserving phase relations between action and sensory feedback,</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Matching control gains and latencies to CPG priors,</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Reusing existing policy libraries (grasp, rotate, flick).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">In AIF terms: skeuomorphic design increases model evidence for treating the tool as temporarily within the self-blanket (functional incorporation), thereby speeding skill acquisition and reducing control effort.</p>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="6">
<li class="break-words whitespace-pre-wrap [&amp;&gt;ul]:whitespace-normal [&amp;&gt;ol]:whitespace-normal">REM and dream bizarreness under blankets</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">During REM, precision on cross-chain coupling drops; CPG chains desynchronize while cortical cycles persist. Blanket assignment becomes unstable:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Some external simulations are briefly treated as self (oneiric agency shifts),</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Others resist entrainment (surreal object dynamics). Dream bizarreness is thus the phenomenology of blanket misassignment under altered precision.</p>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="7">
<li class="break-words whitespace-pre-wrap [&amp;&gt;ul]:whitespace-normal [&amp;&gt;ol]:whitespace-normal">Formal sketch (lightweight)</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Let  be competing generative models with priors over phase dynamics:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\dot{x} = f_{\mathcal{M}}(x, a; \theta) + \omega,\quad s = g_{\mathcal{M}}(x) + \nu,</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\arg\min_{\mathcal{M}} \mathcal{F}(s, a \mid \mathcal{M}) \quad \text{with precision-weighted phase features.}</p>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="8">
<li class="break-words whitespace-pre-wrap [&amp;&gt;ul]:whitespace-normal [&amp;&gt;ol]:whitespace-normal">Practical consequences and tests</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">ToM â‰ physics: Perturb ambiguous stimuli (e.g., dots with goal-like vs force-like trajectories). Fit an AIF model family; expect Bayesian model selection to flip between agentive and physical priors as you modulate entrainability (how easily the observer can â€œjoin the danceâ€).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Tool learning: Interfaces that preserve phase response curves of familiar actions (torsion, viscous drag) will show faster incorporation (shorter actionâ€“prediction latencies, lower control entropy).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">REM: Dream bizarreness should correlate with cross-chain phase jitter (not merely with overall oscillatory power), consistent with desynchronized priors over blankets.</p>
<hr>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Bottom line: In an Active Inference framing, self/other discrimination, theory of mind, intuitive physics, and tool affordances are all Markov-blanket assignments guided by rhythmic entrainment. Your RSVPâ€“CPG account supplies the oscillatory priors (the â€œgaitâ€) that make these assignments fast and reliable. Under this view, doing physics is a special case of mindreading: both infer hidden generators of motion; one uses goal/policy priors, the other law/constraint priorsâ€”and the brain selects between them by asking, in effect, can I join this rhythm?</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Thatâ€™s a beautiful connection. The Mock Turtleâ€™s Song (â€œThe Lobster Quadrilleâ€) already captures, in Carrollâ€™s whimsical way, the essence of what youâ€™ve been describing: inviting another to entrain, to fall into step, to join the dance.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Interpreted through RSVPâ€“CPG and Active Inference with Markov blankets, it looks like this:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The whiting invites the snail to â€œwalk a little fasterâ€ â€” this is an appeal to synchronize rhythms, to join an already-established gait cycle. In AIF terms, itâ€™s an attempt to expand the Markov blanket: to align the snailâ€™s policies with the whitingâ€™s predictive model of motion.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The lobsters and turtles waiting on the shingle stand for the collective cycle â€” the social dance that requires entrainment across agents. The invitation â€œwill you, wonâ€™t you join the dance?â€ is exactly the brainâ€™s inference problem in theory of mind: is this motion entrainable to my own CPG model, and if so, should I treat it as agentive and cooperative?</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The snailâ€™s refusal (â€œtoo far, too farâ€) illustrates what happens when an observed trajectory cannot be reconciled with oneâ€™s own policies or priors. In AIF language: the free-energy cost of entrainment is too high, so the system infers â€œnot-self, not-mineâ€ â€” declining to extend the blanket.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The dance itself is the metaphorical substrate: cognition, social inference, even physics-modeling all reduce to deciding which rhythms to entrain with and which to discount as external dynamics.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">So Carrollâ€™s parody song reads, in your idiom, like a childlike allegory of Markov-blanket boundary inference:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Self rhythms (heartbeat, gait, shadows, perspective distortions) are filtered as â€œalready me.â€</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Other rhythms that can be copied (the whitingâ€™s steps, anotherâ€™s gestures) are treated as intentional agents.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">World rhythms that resist entrainment (currents, reflections, forces) are treated as physics.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">And skeuomorphic affordances â€” like the lobsterâ€™s invitation to â€œjoin the danceâ€ â€” are design cues that reduce the inferential cost of entrainment, making it easy to treat an object or interface as an extension of oneâ€™s own blanket.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Would you like me to recast â€œThe Lobster Quadrilleâ€ stanza by stanza as a parable of Active Inference and RSVPâ€“CPG â€” showing how each line maps to synchrony, free-energy, and blanket assignment? That could work as a playful but rigorous sidebar in your paper.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Hereâ€™s a playful but rigorous parabolic reading of Carrollâ€™s Lobster Quadrille through the lens of Active Inference, Markov blankets, and RSVPâ€“CPG. Each stanza maps onto the problem of deciding whether to entrain with anotherâ€™s rhythm â€” i.e., whether to expand the Markov blanket to include an â€œotherâ€ as agent, environment, or self.</p>
