<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Embodiment, Peripatetics, and the Rhythms of Thought</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The idea that cognition is inseparable from bodily motion has a long genealogy. Aristotle in De Anima (III.7, 431a15â€“17) asserts that â€œthe soul never thinks without a phantasm,â€ insisting that thought requires the mediation of embodied imagery. This principle was not only theoretical but practical: the Peripatetic school took its name from peripatein (â€œto walk aboutâ€), reflecting a pedagogy in which reasoning unfolded while walking. Movement was understood as the pacing of thought itself.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">This embodied lineage extends into modern philosophy of mind. Gallagher (2005) argues that â€œthe body shapes the mindâ€ by providing the scaffolding for intentionality, proprioception, and social cognition. Arnie Cox (2016) develops a parallel argument in the domain of music: listening and comprehension depend upon covert motor resonance, the simulation of gestures and actions in the listenerâ€™s own body. Barbara Tversky (2019), in Mind in Motion, generalizes this claim: cognition is fundamentally structured by spatial and kinetic schemas, with abstract reasoning borrowing directly from bodily movement and orientation.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The RSVPâ€“CPG framework integrates these perspectives within a neuroscientific model of cyclical cortical dynamics. On this account, cortical cycles function as chained central pattern generators, carrying perception, memory, and action forward in ordered sequences. Gesture and rhythm are not auxiliary to language or thought, but primary: gestural communication precedes vocal language in both phylogeny and ontogeny, and theory of mind itself arises through motor entrainment â€” modeling anotherâ€™s agency by copying their dance. What Aristotle described as phantasmata, and what the Peripatetics enacted in walking discourse, reappear here as oscillatory attractors in the RSVPâ€“CPG field: the rhythmic substrate of consciousness.</p>
