<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">RSVP Interpretation of Large-Scale Cortical Cycles (Van Es et al. 2025)</p>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="break-words whitespace-pre-wrap [&amp;&gt;ul]:whitespace-normal [&amp;&gt;ol]:whitespace-normal">Empirical Core</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Using MEG and a new analytic method (TINDA), the team showed that canonical large-scale cortical networks activate in structured cycles of 300â€“1,000 ms.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">These cycles are robust across multiple datasets (UK MEG, Cam-CAN, HCP) and across tasks (rest, memory replay, visual tasks).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Cycle metrics (strength, rate) are heritable and correlate with age, cognition, and reaction time.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Importantly, cycles show asymmetric transitions: some states preferentially follow others, creating broken detailed balanceâ€”a thermodynamic marker of non-equilibrium living systems.</p>
<hr>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="2">
<li class="break-words whitespace-pre-wrap [&amp;&gt;ul]:whitespace-normal [&amp;&gt;ol]:whitespace-normal">RSVP Field Mapping</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">In RSVP, cortical dynamics are expressed in terms of scalar density (Î¦), vector flow (ğ’—), and entropy (S). The Van Es et al. results map naturally:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Î¦ (excitability density): corresponds to the activation amplitude of each network state detected by HMM.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">ğ’— (vector flow): captures asymmetric transition probabilities between states (Aâ†’B â‰  Bâ†’A), the exact structure TINDA quantified.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">S (entropy pacing): cycle strength and rate measure how uncertainty is â€œbudgetedâ€ over time. Stronger cycles = lower entropy drift, but less flexibility.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Thus, the observed cycles are RSVP limit-cycles in Î¦â€“ğ’—â€“S space, with broken detailed balance providing the entropic gradient that keeps the cycle from collapsing into stasis.</p>
<hr>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="3">
<li class="break-words whitespace-pre-wrap [&amp;&gt;ul]:whitespace-normal [&amp;&gt;ol]:whitespace-normal">Lamphronâ€“Lamphrodyne Interpretation</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">RSVPâ€™s lamphronâ€“lamphrodyne framework describes how tensions (lamphrons) between active and inactive networks relax through directed smoothing (lamphrodyne) into the next preferred state.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Each network state is a lamphron well: energy temporarily â€œtrappedâ€ in a configuration.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Asymmetry ensures that lamphrons donâ€™t simply equilibrate, but relax forward into the next network, creating an ongoing cycle.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">This maps directly onto the Oxford teamâ€™s finding of clock-like cortical recurrence: cognition proceeds because lamphrons are continually released by lamphrodynes in ordered fashion.</p>
<hr>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="4">
<li class="break-words whitespace-pre-wrap [&amp;&gt;ul]:whitespace-normal [&amp;&gt;ol]:whitespace-normal">Recursive Causality &amp; Cognitive Windows</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">RSVP emphasizes recursive causality: future constraints modulate present evolution. In the brain:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Cycles create temporal scaffolds: only certain operations (memory replay, motor prep, perception) are optimal at specific cycle phases.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Disruptions (e.g. in dementia, ADHD, or sleep disorders) can be reframed as loss of recursive scaffolding, where Î¦â€“ğ’—â€“S cycles fail to maintain phase-coherence.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">This echoes the Oxford result that cycle phase predicts behavior and memory replay timing.</p>
<hr>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="5">
<li class="break-words whitespace-pre-wrap [&amp;&gt;ul]:whitespace-normal [&amp;&gt;ol]:whitespace-normal">Clinical and AI Implications</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Clinical:
â€“ Cycle slowing with age mirrors RSVPâ€™s prediction that entropic smoothing lengthens under degraded capacity.
â€“ Cycle heritability (rate hÂ² â‰ˆ 0.73) shows RSVPâ€™s entropic budget is genetically constrained.
