<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Do you want me to also walk through how this could be explained to neuroscientists (framing it in terms of standard Bayesian generative models and dynamic systems), or to RSVP insiders (framing it in terms of Î¦â€“ğ’—â€“S fields and lamphron-lamphrodyne dynamics)?</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Got it â€” letâ€™s connect the Oxford paperâ€™s cortical cycles directly to your RSVP-based model of motion, memory, and sleep, where central pattern generators (CPGs) are the backbone. Iâ€™ll keep it in natural language but structured so it bridges the empirical findings with your theoretical system.</p>
<hr>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Cyclical Cortical Networks as RSVPâ€“CPG Chains</p>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="break-words whitespace-pre-wrap [&amp;&gt;ul]:whitespace-normal [&amp;&gt;ol]:whitespace-normal">Motion and CPG Linkages</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">In your framework, motion is generated by chained central pattern generators (CPGs): oscillatory modules that drive repetitive movement patterns (walking, breathing, chewing, etc.).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The Oxford discovery that large-scale cortical networks cycle in a robust, clock-like order can be seen as the cognitive analogue of CPG linkages.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Just as spinal CPGs generate coordinated steps, cortical cycles generate coordinated steps of thought: attention, memory retrieval, perception, motor preparation.</p>
