<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Comparative Analysis: GWT, AIF, and RSVPâ€“CPG</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Theories of consciousness and cognition often rely on metaphors to capture processes too complex for direct observation. Global Workspace Theory (GWT) and Active Inference (AIF) have become two of the most influential, framing cognition respectively as a broadcast system and as a predictive equilibrium machine. The RSVPâ€“CPG framework introduces a third option: cognition as rhythmic gait. This contrast illuminates their mechanisms, metaphors, and implications.</p>
<hr>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Mechanism.
GWT proposes that specialized modules compete for access to a global workspace, with the â€œwinnerâ€ gaining broadcast power across the brain. AIF, by contrast, presents the brain as a predictive engine that continually minimizes variational free energy, unifying perception and action; Baioumy et al. (2020/21) extend this account to robotics, showing that AIF can subsume traditional control architectures like PID controllers. RSVPâ€“CPG breaks from both. Here, cognition is not about winning a contest nor about settling into equilibrium. Instead, it is a sequential, oscillatory process: cortical central pattern generators (CPGs) chain together, with each step smoothing entropy constraints before the next step takes over.</p>
<hr>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Metaphor.
The theories diverge most clearly in metaphor. GWT uses the stage/spotlight image: consciousness as access, with select contents illuminated and broadcast. AIF embraces the equilibrium machine metaphor: cognition as a balancing act, always seeking to minimize surprise. RSVPâ€“CPG advances a movement metaphor: cognition as gait. Here, consciousness does not arise from stasis but from rhythm â€” like walking, where balance comes only through perpetual imbalance, each forward fall caught by the next step. Cognition is not spotlight or balance, but motion.</p>
<hr>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Memory.
GWT treats memory episodically: contents are recalled when they enter the workspace. AIF treats memory statistically: priors embedded in generative models guide inference and action. RSVPâ€“CPG frames memory differently. Memories persist as proxy loops, resonant sub-chains that hitch onto the cortical gait. Like phonological loops repeating items to keep them alive, these proxies cycle through cortical steps, biasing future trajectories and ensuring the past remains folded into the presentâ€™s rhythm.</p>
<hr>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Dynamics of Motion.
In GWT, dynamics are discrete: a representation either ignites and wins access, or it does not. In AIF, dynamics are continuous but teleological: all processes aim toward equilibrium, with prediction errors minimized into balance. RSVPâ€“CPG resists both discreteness and convergence. Its dynamics are oscillatory and non-equilibrium: cognition is sustained forward motion, driven by entropy descent. The point is not to arrive at rest, but to perpetually cycle â€” to keep moving, never settling, always renewing.</p>
<hr>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">REM and Dreaming.
Dreaming reveals each frameworkâ€™s commitments. GWT sees REM as the workspace filling with internal imagery, unchecked by external input. AIF interprets REM as generative models running free, exploring predictions in the absence of sensory correction. RSVPâ€“CPG emphasizes rhythm: in REM, CPG chains desynchronize. The cortical cycle continues, but its gait loses coordination. Out-of-phase loops drift, producing the surreal combinations and disjointed transitions of dream life. Dreams are not failed workspaces or free-running priors but the sound of desynchronized gait.</p>
<hr>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Synthesis.
Viewed together, these three theories offer complementary perspectives. GWT provides a phenomenology of conscious access. AIF supplies the formal grammar of predictive computation. RSVPâ€“CPG contributes the oscillatory skeleton â€” the rhythmic substrate on which both access and prediction rely. By repositioning consciousness not as spotlight or equilibrium but as gait, RSVPâ€“CPG reframes cognition as perpetual motion: an ordered fall through time, a rhythm of steps sustained by entropy itself.</p>
