<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Comparative Analysis: GWT, AIF, and RSVPâ€“CPG</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Theories of consciousness and cognition often rely on metaphors to render tractable processes that resist direct observation. Global Workspace Theory (GWT) and Active Inference (AIF) have emerged as two of the most influential approaches, framing cognition respectively in terms of broadcasting and prediction. The RSVPâ€“CPG framework introduces a third account, in which cognition is modeled as rhythmic gait. Examining the contrasts across these theories illuminates both shared ground and fundamental divergences.</p>
<hr>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Mechanism.
GWT models the brain as a set of specialized modules competing for access to a â€œglobal workspace,â€ with the winning representation broadcast across cortical and subcortical regions (Baars, 1997; Dehaene &amp; Changeux, 2011). AIF, by contrast, posits that cognition arises through continual minimization of variational free energy, unifying perception and action under a single principle (Friston, 2010; Friston et al., 2017). Baioumy et al. (2021) extend this account to robotics, showing how AIF can encompass classic control schemes such as PID loops, enabling manipulators to adapt to disturbances and unmodeled dynamics. RSVPâ€“CPG departs from both: cognition is neither an ignition event nor an equilibrium process, but a sequential unfolding through chained cortical central pattern generators (CPGs). Each step smooths entropy constraints before handing off to the next, producing a continuous rhythm of thought.</p>
<hr>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Metaphor.
The differences are most vivid in metaphor. GWT employs the stage/spotlight image, emphasizing ignition and access (Baars, 1997). AIF is best understood through the metaphor of an equilibrium machine: a system that continuously rebalances itself to minimize surprise (Friston, 2010). RSVPâ€“CPG instead invokes a movement metaphor: cognition as gait. Here, consciousness does not arise from balance or selection, but from rhythmic progression â€” like walking, where stability emerges only through perpetual imbalance, each step caught by the next. Cognition is not spotlight or balance, but forward rhythmic motion.</p>
<hr>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Memory.
Memory is conceptualized differently in each framework. In GWT, memory is episodic, recruited into the workspace when contextually relevant (Dehaene &amp; Naccache, 2001). In AIF, memory is statistical: priors embedded in hierarchical generative models constrain inference (Friston et al., 2017). RSVPâ€“CPG, by contrast, models memory as proxy loops entrained to the cortical gait. These rhythmic subchains act like phonological loops, maintaining resonant traces of past trajectories and biasing future steps. Memory thus persists not through storage or priors, but through ongoing rhythmic resonance.</p>
<hr>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Dynamics of Motion.
In GWT, dynamics are discrete: a representation either wins access or remains local. In AIF, dynamics are continuous but converge toward equilibrium: prediction errors are minimized until the system balances expectations with input (Friston, 2010). RSVPâ€“CPG resists both discreteness and convergence. Its dynamics are oscillatory and non-equilibrium: cognition as perpetual descent along entropy gradients. Like gait, the process is sustained not by reaching stability but by repeatedly â€œfalling forwardâ€ into the next phase.</p>
<hr>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">REM and Dreaming.
Each framework also offers an account of paradoxical sleep. GWT interprets REM dreams as the workspace being populated by internally generated imagery in the absence of external input (Dehaene, 2014). AIF views REM as the generative model free-running, testing predictions unconstrained by sensory correction (Hobson &amp; Friston, 2012). RSVPâ€“CPG instead highlights rhythm: in REM, CPG chains desynchronize. The cortical cycle continues, but its gait becomes misaligned. Out-of-phase loops drift, producing the surreal juxtapositions of dreams. Dreams are thus not failed workspaces or unconstrained priors, but the experiential signature of desynchronized gait.</p>
<hr>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Synthesis.
Taken together, these three frameworks provide complementary perspectives. GWT offers a phenomenological account of conscious access. AIF provides the statistical grammar of predictive computation. RSVPâ€“CPG supplies the oscillatory skeleton â€” the rhythmic substrate on which both broadcasting and prediction ride. By repositioning consciousness not as spotlight or equilibrium but as gait, RSVPâ€“CPG reframes cognition as perpetual motion: an ordered fall through time, sustained by entropy itself.</p>
