<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Hereâ€™s a clean follow-up section you could add right after the REM comparison, sharpening the overall positioning:</p>
<hr>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Beyond REM: Explaining the Limits of GWT and AIF</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The REM case study highlights more than a difference in explanatory flavor: it exposes the structural limits of both Global Workspace Theory and Active Inference. GWT offers a phenomenological description of conscious access, but its stage/spotlight metaphor lacks a mechanism for why contents fall out of sync in REM or why cognition unfolds rhythmically even when the â€œspotlightâ€ is absent. AIF, meanwhile, provides a mathematically rigorous account of predictive regulation, yet its commitment to equilibrium means it struggles to account for paradoxical regimes where synchronization deliberately fails, such as REM sleep or certain dissociative states.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The RSVPâ€“CPG framework fills this gap by providing the missing oscillatory skeleton. Cycles and chains are not accidents layered atop a predictive engine or a broadcast stage; they are the rhythmic substrate that makes both possible. Broadcasting can be understood as a momentary alignment of chains that amplifies one content; prediction-error minimization can be reframed as the entropic pacing that constrains how far chains drift from synchrony. In this sense, RSVPâ€“CPG does not merely complement GWT and AIF but explains why they work at all â€” and why they break down under conditions like REM.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">By foregrounding rhythm and desynchronization, RSVPâ€“CPG recasts consciousness not as equilibrium or spotlight, but as gait: a perpetual oscillatory movement that both supports and exceeds the frameworks of global access and predictive coding.</p>
