<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Conclusion: Cycles as the Common Ground</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The discovery that large-scale cortical networks follow robust cyclical activation patterns (Van Es et al., 2025) reframes the debate between Global Workspace Theory, Active Inference, and RSVPâ€“CPG. GWT captures the phenomenology of access but has no intrinsic account of why representations appear and fade in rhythmic order. AIF formalizes the logic of prediction but struggles to explain why cognition is paced by oscillations rather than converging smoothly to equilibrium. RSVPâ€“CPG, in contrast, predicts precisely this kind of cycle: cognition as gait, an ordered fall through time sustained by chained oscillators that smooth entropy constraints.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Our Bayesian analysis of MEG data situates the Oxford groupâ€™s observed cycles as evidence of this rhythmic substrate. Cycle strength and rate emerge naturally from the interplay of cortical asymmetries, CPG chain synchrony, and entropy pacing. Memory replay, reaction time, and even REM desynchronization become measurable expressions of how these cycles entrain or drift. Where GWT and AIF offer metaphors of spotlight and equilibrium, RSVPâ€“CPG provides the missing mechanism: the oscillatory skeleton that makes those metaphors possible.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">In this light, cortical cycles are not just a new empirical curiosity but the structural proof of principle that cognition is organized as a gait. They show that the brain is not a theater, nor a prediction engine alone, but a rhythmic walker in state space, forever falling forward into the next step of thought.</p>
