<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Recent evidence that large-scale cortical networks exhibit robust cyclical activation patterns (Van Es et al., 2025) underscores the importance of oscillatory attractors in the organization of cognition. Global Workspace Theory provides a phenomenological framework for conscious access but does not specify why cortical transitions occur in structured cycles. Active Inference offers a formal account of predictive regulation but presupposes equilibrium-seeking dynamics, leaving paradoxical regimes such as REM sleep insufficiently explained.</p>
