<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\paragraph</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Bayesian calibration.} Estimate </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">(</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Omega</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">,</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\gamma</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">,</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\eta</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">)</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> from FO-asymmetry matrices and cycle strength; recover </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">S</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> by regressing cycle rate/strength and cross-layer coherence; fit ACE on </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\log</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> rate to quantify </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">h^</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">2</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">, mirroring the empirical pipeline </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\citep</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{van2025cortical}.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\section</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Bayesian Analysis for RSVP-Framed Cortical Cycles}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">We present a Bayesian generative model that treats the Oxford MEG result as data, linking it to the RSVP limit-cycle model. (Detailed code and parameters omitted for brevity; see supplemental for Stan/NumPyro implementation.)</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\section</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Empirical Testability: Protocols and Predictions}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">(Details of empirical protocols as provided in the conversation history, structured in enumerated list.)</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\section</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Conclusion: Oscillatory Attractors as the Substrate of Cognition}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Recent evidence that large-scale cortical networks exhibit robust cyclical activation patterns </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\citep</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{van2025cortical} underscores the importance of oscillatory attractors in the organization of cognition. Global Workspace Theory provides a phenomenological framework for conscious access but does not specify why cortical transitions occur in structured cycles. Active Inference offers a formal account of predictive regulation but presupposes equilibrium-seeking dynamics, leaving paradoxical regimes such as REM sleep insufficiently explained.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">The RSVP--CPG framework situates cortical cycles as the fundamental substrate: cognition emerges from chained oscillatory processes that enforce ordered transitions across functional networks. Cycles operate as non-equilibrium limit cycles, maintaining progression through recursive smoothing of entropic constraints. Our Bayesian reformulation of the Van Es et al. findings formalizes these dynamics by estimating global cycle order, asymmetry, and rate as parameters of coupled oscillator systems. This approach generates empirically testable predictions: (i) cycle strength will covary with working memory precision, (ii) cycle desynchronization will track REM-specific dream phenomenology, and (iii) heritability of cycle rate will reflect genetic modulation of oscillator coupling parameters.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">By identifying cortical cycles as Lyapunov-stable attractors rather than incidental correlates, RSVP--CPG provides the oscillatory skeleton absent from both GWT and AIF. This reframing advances a unified account in which conscious access, predictive regulation, and dream phenomenology are all manifestations of a deeper rhythmic substrate.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
