<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\section*</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Future Work}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">The framework developed here connects predictive geometry, sparse inference, attention dynamics, and embodied generative loops into a single account of linguistic and cognitive emergence. This synthesis opens multiple empirical, theoretical, and engineering directions. We group future work into five tightly-coupled research agendas.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\subsection*</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{1. Geometry of Predictive Manifolds}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">This paper formalizes semantic space as a Riemannian manifold endowed with the Fisher--Rao metric. Immediate research extensions include:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">itemize</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> Computing </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">curvature spectra</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} (Ricci, sectional, and scalar curvature) of next-token manifolds in pretrained LLMs, and correlating them with reasoning complexity, surprisal profiles, and compositional reuse.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> Characterizing </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">phase transitions</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} in manifold geometry across scale (model size, data quantity, training time) to determine whether reasoning ability corresponds to curvature flattening, dimensional collapse, or geodesic sparsification.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> Evaluating whether human incremental sentence processing follows </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">shortest-path geodesics</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} in model manifolds, via eyetracking or self-paced reading datasets.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> Testing whether logical contradiction, metaphor, and semantic anomaly occupy </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">negative-curvature regions</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}, predicting longer geodesics and higher predictive entropy.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">itemize</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\subsection*</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{2. Mechanistic Tests of Auto-Autoregression}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Barenholtzâ€™s ``auto-autoregressive'' hypothesis predicts that learned predictors internalize their own continuation machinery. Future tests include:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">itemize</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> Identifying whether transformers implement </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">internal hypothesis enumeration loops</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} during multi-step reasoning, detectable via recurrent attention motifs or cyclic state visitation.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> Probing for </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">implicit world-simulators</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} by ablating components suspected to store reusable transition templates and measuring collapses in sample efficiency.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> Testing whether successive transformer layers implement </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">iterative posterior sharpening</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} akin to belief propagation or Kalman-style smoothing.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> Measuring representational </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">hysteresis</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} across long-horizon predictions to detect when a model becomes ``committed'' to a latent trajectory.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">itemize</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\subsection*</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{3. Biological and Developmental Validation}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">The cognitive interpretation of predictive geometry makes strong claims about human learning, none of which require language-specific priors. Experiments to evaluate this include:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">itemize</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> Comparing child and model learning curves on </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">synthetic holographic-linguistic agnostic grammars</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} to test whether both rely on sparse template reuse rather than symbolic rule induction.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> Measuring whether human learners show </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">sharp posterior collapse</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} onto a small set of continuations during comprehension, via information-theoretic analysis of eye movement entropy.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> Testing whether infants exhibit </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">geodesic bias</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} in sequence learning tasksâ€”preferring low-curvature transitions over high-curvature, even when transition probability is controlled.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> Using MEG/EEG decoding to evaluate whether neural next-word prediction signatures exhibit </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">natural gradient-like error propagation</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">itemize</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\subsection*</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{4. Scaling Laws and Efficiency Constraints}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">If language learning is sparse predictive inference, then efficiencyâ€”not data sizeâ€”determines asymptotic behavior. Future work will examine:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">itemize</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> Whether scaling laws break when </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">posterior sparsity saturates</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}, predicting a regime where additional parameters add diminishing linguistic generalization.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> Training small models with explicit </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">sparsity penalties on predictive hypothesis support</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}, testing whether this yields large-model generalization benchmarks with fewer parameters.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> Measuring </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">sample efficiency as a function of manifold intrinsic dimensionality</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} rather than parameter count.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">itemize</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\subsection*</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{5. Interpretability and Causal Interventions}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">The geometric hypothesis yields unusually precise mechanistic probes:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">itemize</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">Geodesic intervention</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}: steering hidden states along low-curvature directions and testing whether reasoning success rates improve.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">Curvature surgery</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}: artificially flattening or sharpening local manifold curvature and evaluating effects on logical consistency, hallucination rate, and compositionality.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">Posterior-editing interventions</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}: rewriting internal attention distributions to match the analytically-derived sparse posterior, and measuring alignment with Bayes-optimal predictions.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">Counterfactual gradient routing</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}: forcing updates along or orthogonal to estimated Fisher directions to test whether learning performance is governed by natural-gradient alignment.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">itemize</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">---</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\subsection*</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Grand Challenge Questions}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">These projects collectively aim to resolve a deeper scientific question:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">center</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\textit</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230); font-style: italic;">Is language intelligence the discovery of latent generative geometry under constraints of sparse continuation, rather than the acquisition of syntactic rules or symbolic primitives?</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">center</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">If correct, the consequences extend beyond linguistics and AI. Language would cease to be a special cognitive module or a cultural artifact encoded in the brain, and instead be understood as the </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">unavoidable equilibrium</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} of any sufficiently efficient system trained to recursively predict itself under limited bandwidth.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\section</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Conclusion: Oscillatory Attractors as the Substrate of Cognition}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Recent evidence that large-scale cortical networks exhibit robust cyclical activation patterns </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\citep</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{van2025cortical} underscores the importance of oscillatory attractors in the organization of cognition. Global Workspace Theory provides a phenomenological framework for conscious access but does not specify why cortical transitions occur in structured cycles. Active Inference offers a formal account of predictive regulation but presupposes equilibrium-seeking dynamics, leaving paradoxical regimes such as REM sleep insufficiently explained.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">The RSVP--CPG framework situates cortical cycles as the fundamental substrate: cognition emerges from chained oscillatory processes that enforce ordered transitions across functional networks. These cycles operate as non-equilibrium limit cycles, maintaining progression through recursive smoothing of entropic constraints. Our Bayesian reformulation of the Van Es et al. findings formalizes these dynamics by estimating global cycle order, asymmetry, and rate as parameters of coupled oscillator systems. This approach generates empirically testable predictions: (i) cycle strength will covary with working memory precision, (ii) cycle desynchronization will track REM-specific dream phenomenology, and (iii) heritability of cycle rate will reflect genetic modulation of oscillator coupling parameters.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">By identifying cortical cycles as Lyapunov-stable attractors rather than incidental correlates, RSVP--CPG provides the oscillatory skeleton absent from both GWT and AIF. This reframing advances a unified account in which conscious access, predictive regulation, and dream phenomenology are all manifestations of a deeper rhythmic substrate.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\bibliographystyle</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{apalike}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\bibliography</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{consciousness_as_gait}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">document</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span></code></pre></div><div></div><div class="false flex flex-col gap-2 h-full"></div></div></div>
